Subject: [PATCH 2/4] tpm: Add a TPM-resident key to authenticate hibernation
 images

Add tpm2_hibernation_mac(), which computes an HMAC-SHA256 of a digest
with a key that never leaves the TPM, and tpm2_hibernation_supported(),
which tells whether the default TPM can do so.

The key is a keyed-hash primary object of the owner hierarchy, so the
same template derives the same key at every boot without storing
anything, until the owner hierarchy is cleared. It cannot be used with
a password: its only authorization is a PolicyPCR on PCR 23 at the
value that a reset followed by one extend with a fixed label gives.
CONFIG_TCG_TPM_RESTRICT_PCR keeps every other user of the TPM from
resetting or extending PCR 23, so that value exists only between the
opening and the closing extends of tpm2_hibernation_mac(), which holds
the TPM throughout and is meant to run while user space is frozen. If
PCR 23 cannot be moved away from that value, the TPM is disabled until
the next boot rather than left usable with the key authorized.

Unlike a key unsealed into kernel memory, this key cannot be read back
through /proc/kcore or a BPF program, which kernel lockdown allows in
integrity mode, so it holds without confidentiality mode.

The helper is selected by the hibernation code in a following change.

